From: hawkff <109485367+hawkff@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Tue, 24 Nov 2026 23:18:59 +0000
Subject: Enable the back/forward cache in regular profiles on 4 GB devices

A back or forward navigation in a regular profile can restore the
previous page from memory instead of loading it again. The cache keeps
the upstream Android configuration: up to 6 pages per tab for at most 10
minutes, fewer under memory pressure.

The cache stays off on devices that report 3276 MiB of memory or less,
approximately the 3.2 GiB boundary that base::SysInfo uses between 3 GB
and 4 GB devices. The kernel reports less than the installed memory, so
this admits devices sold with 4 GB and excludes those sold with 3 GB.
Cached pages keep their renderer processes alive, which raises memory
use; under memory pressure those processes can be killed, making some
history navigations slower. The limit is the default of the
memory_threshold_for_back_forward_cache_in_mb parameter of
BackForwardCacheMemoryControls, raised from 1200 to 3276 MB.

Off-the-record profiles (incognito tabs, always-incognito mode, incognito
and ephemeral custom tabs) never store pages in the cache:
TabWebContentsDelegateAndroid reports the cache as unsupported for them.

A page keeps what was decided when it was loaded: the renderer content
settings, the renderer process with its JIT and optimizer mode, its
service and shared workers, whether the ad blocker filters its requests,
and its local network access policy. When one of those site settings
changes, including a site's cookie setting, which also decides whether
third-party workers may run, or when all site settings change at once
(for example a policy update), every page of the profile is marked as
not cacheable and pages already in the cache are evicted. A later back
navigation then loads the page again with the new setting, so a revoked
site permission cannot come back through a restored page. The global
third-party cookie setting is not a site setting: like an open page, a
cached page keeps the third-party workers it already has after that
setting changes.

The ad blocker skips element hiding for documents restored from the
cache; they keep what was applied when they were loaded.

